Contents

INTRODUCTION
This is the second in the series of thematic publications comprising statistical information from the 
Census of Population and Housing 2021. The Census had 21 November 2021 as reference day, with 
compilation and analysis carried out in 2022. The Preliminary Report was published in August 2022 and 
the first volume of finalised results in February 2023. The first volume contained a range of statistics 
such as population, migration, citizenship, racial origin, and religious affiliation. This second volume 
focuses on dwellings and includes information on the different types of dwellings, overall condition, 
type of tenure, dwelling membership, and facilities in the main residence. This introduction presents 
extracts from the findings.
Dwelling stock
• A growth of 7,345 dwellings per year was observed during the past decade. Over the century 1921-2021, the dwelling 
stock more than quadrupled.
• This is the largest intercensal growth in dwellings ever recorded.
• The top three localities recording increases in dwelling stock over the Census of 2011 were: San Pawl il-Baħar, L-Imsida 
and Il-Mosta. 
• Valletta, L-Isla and San Lawrenz in Gozo saw the most pronounced decreases.
Type of dwelling
• In many localities the majority of housing stock were flats or penthouses. The top localities in this regard were San Pawl 
il-Baħar, Ta’ Xbiex, and San Ġiljan where flats and penthouses made up over four-fifths of housing stock. 
• Terraced houses were particularly in evidence in Ta’ Kerċem and Il-Fontana in Gozo and L-Imdina. But the proportions 
representing terraced houses of total dwelling stock in these three localities were appreciably lower than the proportions 
representing flats and penthouses in the afore-mentioned San Pawl il-Baħar, Ta’Xbiex, and San Ġiljan.
Main residential dwelling
• The proportion of dwellings classified as main residences was 72.5 per cent. In the 2011 Census, main residences. 
comprised 68.2 per cent of housing stock.
• The two most prevalent facilities installed in the main dwelling emerged as the internet and air conditioning systems, both 
seeing a considerable rise over the last Census in 2011. The district of Gozo and Comino was in the lead regarding home 
facilities such as solar water heaters and reverse osmosis systems.
• In contrast, just over one-fourth of main residences had the ‘traditional’ facility of a well, down from 35.9 per cent of main 
residences recorded in the Census of 2011.
Secondary, seasonally-used or vacant dwelling
• This category made up 27.5 per cent of housing stock compared to the 2011 proportion of 31.8 per cent.
• Around two-thirds of secondary, seasonally-used or vacant properties were flats and penthouses while about one-fourth 
of this category were terraced houses.  
• The proportion of secondary, seasonally-used or vacant dwellings in a bad condition was much higher than the main 
dwelling counterpart. In fact, 8.1 per cent of this category emerged as needing significant repair work.
MR ETIENNE CARUANA
Census Officer
Introduction
Home ownership vs rent
• The Census showed that nearly 75 per cent of Maltese individuals owned their main residence.
• In contrast, 52.8 per cent of non-Maltese residents rented furnished accommodation.
• Residences rented on a furnished basis have been spiralling upwards since the early 2000s, to reach a 36.5 per cent 
proportion of all main residences in 2016 and thereafter. Conversely, rentals of unfurnished main residences declined 
consistently.
• The monthly median rent payment for furnished main residences was calculated at €700 but varied with the location. 
• Generally, the majority of Maltese individuals who rented their residences had contracts of three or more years, contrasting 
with the majority of non-Maltese who held rental contracts of less than two years.
Household members
• One of the significant phenomena to come out of Census 2021 is the increasing trend in single-member main residences. 
These accounted for around one-third of total main residences, close to the proportions of two-member and three plus-
member households.
• Disparities also emerged when comparing the membership of Maltese and non-Maltese main residences. The average 
dwelling having only Maltese members was 2.44 in size, while the average dwelling consisting solely of non-Maltese had 
1.98 persons residing there.
Acknowledgements
The above observations represent only a fraction of the Census results. These points can perhaps be expected to strike the eye 
more than others. However, all the Census data is valid and interesting and can offer the researcher big opportunities to form 
insights on how people and the living environment in Malta and Gozo are developing. My thanks are due to the Census Office and 
the Methodology and Quality Unit within the NSO which compiled the tables and undertook the analysis, led by Silvan Zammit 
and Matthew Zerafa, the two Deputy Census Officers. A special mention is due to the public entities, organisations, and NGOs 
which, prior to the Census, participated in the public consultation and subsequently made available their administrative registers 
for the required validations. My ultimate thanks must go to the resident population of Malta whose response was crucial to the 
success of Census 2021.
Etienne CARUANA
Census Officer 
August 2023
